tc2-rp2040-logs: decode in a child process so a corrupt stream cannot kill the service

defmt-decoder trusts the u32 element count of a `{=[?]}` argument and calls
Vec::with_capacity() with it before reading any elements. A desynchronised RTT
stream can therefore make it request gigabytes (seen in production as
`memory allocation of 6758072480 bytes failed`), and Rust aborts the whole
process on allocation failure, which cannot be caught.

Run the decoder in a child process (this binary with --decode). The parent
keeps openocd and the RTT socket, remembers the last 256 raw bytes, and on a
child crash logs them and starts a fresh decoder. Five crashes in a minute
restart the whole capture instead.

